<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Johnson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1709–1784</span> · Also known as Dr Johnson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Johnson was an English writer, critic and lexicographer. His Dictionary of the English Language of 1755, compiled largely alone over nine years, was the most influential English dictionary until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> English Dictionary. He also wrote poetry, essays, the novella Rasselas and the Lives of the Poets, and his friend James Boswell's Life of Johnson made him one of the best-known figures of his age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson.jpg" alt="Samuel Johnson" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Johnson_by_Joshua_Reynolds.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
